    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d787">

                <pb facs="0861" n="757" sameAs="frus:frus1932v05/pg757"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/29729: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Clark</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1932-06-16T13:00:00-06:00">June 16, 1932—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>104. Referring to my telegram No. 103, June 13 [<hi rendition="#italic">15</hi>],
                    11 a.m., 1932.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d787-f1"/>
                </p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Minister Téllez informs me that General Calles and wife leave here today
                        for the United States via Laredo, destination New York,—whether on regular
                        Laredo train leaving this evening or on a special train this afternoon seems
                        not decided.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>Foreign Office states that they are taking up matter through the Mexican
                        Embassy in Washington.</item>
                    <label>3.</label>
                    <item>I will telegraph details as soon as I can obtain them, and will likewise
                        advise border.</item>
                    <label>4.</label>
                    <item>Recalling the Valls situation at Laredo<anchor n="27" xml:id="d787-f2"/> I
                        respectfully urge that a military guard of honor from Fort McIntosh be used
                        to escort General Calles and wife through Texas (see Department’s telegram
                        541, December 14, 1929).<anchor n="28" xml:id="d787-f3"/>
                    </item>
                </list>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Clark</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
